1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,560
My friends, I once told a divided nation that a house divided against itself cannot stand.
朋友们，我曾对一个分裂的国家说：裂开的房子站立不住。

2
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:10,258
Tonight I bring you a different house.
今晚，我要讲另一座房子。

3
00:00:10,708 --> 00:00:16,722
It is made of muscle, and it is not divided by war, but by a beam of light you cannot see.
它由肌肉筑成；撕裂它的不是战争，而是一束你看不见的光。

4
00:00:17,172 --> 00:00:22,073
Picture a woman who has just finished radiotherapy for a sarcoma in her thigh.
设想一位女士，刚因大腿肉瘤做完放疗。

5
00:00:22,523 --> 00:00:27,884
The tumor is gone. The doctors shake her hand, and she goes home.
肿瘤消失了。医生和她握手道贺，她回了家。

6
00:00:28,334 --> 00:00:33,106
Five years later, she finds she cannot climb the stairs as she once did.
五年后，她发现自己爬楼梯不如从前了。

7
00:00:33,556 --> 00:00:40,213
The muscle near the old tumor has grown thin, and it has grown hard, like leather left too long in the sun.
旧肿瘤附近的肌肉变细了，也变硬了，像在太阳下晒得太久的皮革。

8
00:00:40,663 --> 00:00:47,164
Her story is a composite, but doctors who care for cancer survivors will tell you it is a familiar one.
她的故事是一个综合的示意病例，但照顾肿瘤幸存者的医生会告诉你，这样的故事并不陌生。

9
00:00:47,614 --> 00:00:52,546
That muscle was never the target. It was only standing nearby.
那块肌肉从来不是靶子，它只是站在旁边。

10
00:00:52,996 --> 00:00:54,726
So here is our case.
所以，这就是我们的案子。

11
00:00:55,176 --> 00:01:01,382
Why does a muscle that was merely near the battle grow weak and stiff, years after the guns fell silent?
一块只是靠近战场的肌肉，为什么会在硝烟散尽多年之后，变弱、变硬？

12
00:01:01,832 --> 00:01:07,141
In this series, we will work that case the way a country lawyer argues before a jury.
在这个系列里，我们会像一位乡村律师在陪审团面前辩护那样办这个案子。

13
00:01:07,591 --> 00:01:09,798
One piece of evidence at a time.
一条证据，接着一条证据。

14
00:01:10,248 --> 00:01:16,855
And I will tell you plainly which pieces are proven, which are only suggestive, and which are still guesses.
我也会坦白告诉你，哪些已被证明，哪些只是提示，哪些仍是猜测。

15
00:01:17,305 --> 00:01:24,258
Watch the corner of the screen. A green tag marks a fact, shown by direct experiment.
请留意画面角落。绿色标签代表事实，由直接实验证明。

16
00:01:24,708 --> 00:01:31,537
An amber tag marks an association, two things that travel together without proof that one causes the other.
琥珀色标签代表关联：两件事总是一起出现，但还没证明谁导致了谁。

17
00:01:31,987 --> 00:01:37,543
And a red tag marks a hypothesis, an idea still waiting for its day in court.
红色标签代表假说：一个仍在等待出庭受审的想法。

18
00:01:37,993 --> 00:01:45,798
Tonight, in the first episode, we follow the bullet from the moment it leaves the machine to the moment it reaches the blueprint of life.
今晚第一集，我们追踪这颗子弹：从它离开机器的那一刻，到它击中生命蓝图的那一刻。

19
00:01:49,248 --> 00:01:51,651
Let us begin at the moment of impact.
先从击中的那一刻讲起。

20
00:01:52,101 --> 00:02:02,047
Radiotherapy uses X-rays. They are particles of light, called photons, carrying far more energy than the light by which you read.
放疗用的是 X 射线。它们是光的粒子，叫光子，携带的能量远远超过你读书时的那种光。

21
00:02:02,497 --> 00:02:08,096
When such a photon passes through tissue, it knocks electrons clean out of atoms.
这样的光子穿过组织时，会把电子从原子里整个撞出去。

22
00:02:08,546 --> 00:02:15,124
Those flying electrons then knock out more electrons, like one billiard ball scattering a whole rack.
飞出去的电子又撞出更多电子，就像一颗台球撞散了整盘球。

23
00:02:15,574 --> 00:02:22,205
That is what the word ionizing means. It leaves behind atoms that are charged and broken.
这就是“电离”的意思：留下带电的、破损的原子。

24
00:02:22,655 --> 00:02:28,132
Now remember a plain fact about the body. A cell is mostly water.
再记住身体的一个朴素事实：细胞的大部分是水。

25
00:02:28,582 --> 00:02:34,300
So the bullet most often strikes not the DNA itself, but the water that surrounds it.
所以这颗子弹最常击中的，不是 DNA 本身，而是包围着它的水。

26
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:40,046
When water is split by radiation, it makes fragments called free radicals.
水被辐射劈开后，会生成叫“自由基”的碎片。

27
00:02:40,496 --> 00:02:43,644
The most dangerous of them is the hydroxyl radical.
其中最危险的，是羟自由基。

28
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:49,857
It lives for only a tiny fraction of a second, and it travels only a few nanometers.
它只存在极短的一瞬，只能移动几纳米。

29
00:02:50,307 --> 00:02:57,354
But within that short reach, it tears at whatever it touches, and DNA is a large and tempting target.
但在这么短的距离内，它碰到什么就撕裂什么，而 DNA 是一个又大又诱人的靶子。

30
00:02:57,804 --> 00:03:02,260
Scientists call these two routes direct action and indirect action.
科学家把这两条路称为直接作用和间接作用。

31
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:06,831
In direct action, the radiation hits the DNA itself.
直接作用，是射线直接打中 DNA。

32
00:03:07,281 --> 00:03:12,644
In indirect action, the radiation hits water, and the radicals do the damage.
间接作用，是射线打中水，再由自由基造成损伤。

33
00:03:13,094 --> 00:03:16,861
For X-rays, the indirect route does most of the work.
对 X 射线来说，大部分损伤走的是间接这条路。

34
00:03:17,311 --> 00:03:23,635
Oxygen makes matters worse, because it helps fix the damage in place before the cell can undo it.
氧气会让情况更糟，因为它会在细胞来得及撤销之前，把损伤“固定”下来。

35
00:03:24,085 --> 00:03:29,593
How much radiation are we talking about? We measure the dose in grays.
我们说的剂量有多大？剂量的单位叫戈瑞，写作 Gy。

36
00:03:30,043 --> 00:03:35,027
One gray means one joule of energy absorbed by each kilogram of tissue.
1 Gy 的意思是：每千克组织吸收 1 焦耳的能量。

37
00:03:35,477 --> 00:03:39,551
That is a tiny amount of heat, too small to warm your hand.
这点热量小得可怜，连你的手都暖不了。

38
00:03:40,001 --> 00:03:43,281
Yet a few grays to the whole body can kill a person.
可是全身受照几 Gy，就足以致命。

39
00:03:43,731 --> 00:03:48,770
The danger is not in the heat. It is in where the energy lands.
危险不在热量，而在能量落在了哪里。

40
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,794
In the clinic, doctors rarely give one large dose.
在临床上，医生很少一次给一个大剂量。

41
00:03:53,244 --> 00:03:59,847
They divide it into many small daily doses, often about two grays each, over several weeks.
他们把它分成许多次每天的小剂量，常见每次约 2 Gy，持续数周。

42
00:04:00,297 --> 00:04:05,786
Healthy tissue gets time to repair between visits, while many tumors repair less well.
正常组织在两次治疗之间有时间修复，而许多肿瘤修复得更差。

43
00:04:06,236 --> 00:04:14,451
Experiments in mice show that the same total dose, given in one blow or in several fractions, remodels muscle differently.
小鼠实验显示，同样的总剂量，一次给完和分几次给，对肌肉的重塑并不相同。

44
00:04:14,901 --> 00:04:20,896
To compare such schedules, scientists use a number called the biologically effective dose.
为了比较这些方案，科学家使用一个叫“生物有效剂量”的数字，简称 BED。

45
00:04:21,346 --> 00:04:27,938
So keep this in your pocket, for it will matter in every episode. Dose makes the poison.
所以请把这句话揣在口袋里，它在每一集都用得上：剂量决定毒性。

46
00:04:28,388 --> 00:04:36,880
A recent study found that very low doses, in the range of a CT scan, did not harm muscle stem cells growing in a dish.
最近一项研究发现，CT 扫描量级的极低剂量，并没有伤害培养皿里的肌肉干细胞。

47
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:45,304
A rat given ninety grays in one shot, and a patient given sixty grays over thirty visits, are not living the same story.
一次吃下 90 Gy 的大鼠，和分 30 次接受 60 Gy 的病人，经历的不是同一个故事。

48
00:04:45,754 --> 00:04:53,118
When we read a paper, our first question will always be: what dose, in what animal, and over how long?
所以每读一篇论文，我们的第一个问题永远是：多大剂量？什么动物？多长时间？

49
00:04:56,568 --> 00:04:59,028
Now to the target that matters most.
现在来看最要紧的靶子。

50
00:04:59,478 --> 00:05:06,710
Every cell keeps its blueprint in DNA: two strands wound together, each strand a mirror copy of the other.
每个细胞都把蓝图存放在 DNA 里：两条链缠在一起，彼此互为镜像副本。

51
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:12,473
Think of it as a great book of building plans, printed twice on facing pages.
你可以把它想成一本巨大的建筑图纸，每一页都在对开页上印了两遍。

52
00:05:12,923 --> 00:05:18,816
Radiation damages that book in many ways. It can smudge a single letter.
辐射会以多种方式损坏这本书。它可以弄脏一个字母。

53
00:05:19,266 --> 00:05:23,364
It can cut one strand, which we call a single-strand break.
它可以切断一条链，我们叫它单链断裂。

54
00:05:23,814 --> 00:05:29,707
Or it can cut both strands, close together, so that the blueprint falls into two pieces.
或者在相近的位置把两条链都切断，让蓝图断成两截。

55
00:05:30,157 --> 00:05:32,257
That is the double-strand break.
这就是双链断裂。

56
00:05:32,707 --> 00:05:36,211
When only one strand is cut, repair is easy.
如果只断一条链，修复很容易。

57
00:05:36,661 --> 00:05:43,629
The cell reads the intact strand and fills in the gap, the way you might restore a torn page from its carbon copy.
细胞照着完好的那条链把缺口补上，就像用复写纸副本修好撕破的一页。

58
00:05:44,079 --> 00:05:49,309
But when both strands are cut at the same spot, there is no copy left to read there.
可如果两条链在同一处都断了，那个位置就没有副本可读了。

59
00:05:49,759 --> 00:05:54,870
Worse, the two loose ends can drift apart, or be joined to the wrong partner.
更糟的是，两个松脱的断端可能漂开，甚至被接到错误的伙伴上。

60
00:05:55,320 --> 00:06:03,651
In 1990, John Ward gathered the evidence of many laboratories into a review that radiation biologists still cite today.
1990 年，John Ward 把许多实验室的证据汇总成一篇综述，放射生物学家至今仍在引用。

61
00:06:04,101 --> 00:06:06,869
Single-strand breaks are far more common.
单链断裂要常见得多。

62
00:06:07,319 --> 00:06:13,387
Yet the double-strand break, though rarer, is the lesion most closely tied to a cell's death.
然而双链断裂虽然少，却是和细胞死亡关系最密切的损伤。

63
00:06:13,837 --> 00:06:18,070
Ward also stressed that radiation tends to cluster its damage.
Ward 还强调，辐射造成的损伤往往成簇出现。

64
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:24,877
Several injuries land within a few turns of the helix, like a volley of shot rather than a single ball.
几处伤落在螺旋的寥寥几圈之内，像是一阵霰弹，而不是一颗子弹。

65
00:06:25,327 --> 00:06:32,370
Clustered damage is harder to mend, and that is part of what makes radiation so different from ordinary wear and tear.
成簇的损伤更难修好，这正是辐射和日常磨损截然不同的原因之一。

66
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:36,869
But how do we see a break that is only a few billionths of a meter wide?
可是，宽度只有几十亿分之一米的断口，我们怎么看得见？

67
00:06:37,319 --> 00:06:39,440
Here the detectives found a flare.
在这里，侦探们找到了一枚信号弹。

68
00:06:39,890 --> 00:06:49,927
In 1998, Emmy Rogakou and her colleagues showed that within minutes of a double-strand break, a histone protein called H2AX changes.
1998 年，Emmy Rogakou 和同事证明：双链断裂发生后几分钟内，一种叫 H2AX 的组蛋白会发生变化。

69
00:06:50,377 --> 00:06:54,008
Histones are the spools around which DNA is wound.
组蛋白，就是 DNA 缠绕其上的线轴。

70
00:06:54,458 --> 00:07:00,624
Near the break, H2AX receives a phosphate tag on serine one hundred thirty-nine.
在断口附近，H2AX 的第 139 位丝氨酸被加上一个磷酸标签。

71
00:07:01,074 --> 00:07:08,717
The tagged form is called gamma-H2AX, and it spreads along the DNA for a great distance around the break.
这种带标签的形式叫 γH2AX，它会沿着断口两侧的 DNA 铺开很长一段。

72
00:07:09,167 --> 00:07:14,883
Stain it with a glowing antibody, and every break becomes a bright dot under the microscope.
用发光的抗体去染它，每个断口在显微镜下都变成一个亮点。

73
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:18,078
Count the dots, and you count the breaks.
数亮点，就是数断口。

74
00:07:18,528 --> 00:07:22,666
Watch the dots fade over hours, and you are watching repair.
看着亮点在几个小时里慢慢消退，你看到的就是修复。

75
00:07:23,116 --> 00:07:28,698
Nearly every paper on radiation and muscle that we will meet in this series uses this flare.
这个系列里我们会读到的辐射与肌肉论文，几乎都用到了这枚信号弹。

76
00:07:29,148 --> 00:07:33,489
If you are new to the laboratory, it may be the first stain you learn.
如果你刚进实验室，它很可能是你学会的第一种染色。

77
00:07:33,939 --> 00:07:39,808
Now, a note from the bench, for those of you who will soon do this work with your own hands.
接下来是一段“实验台笔记”，送给马上要亲手做这些实验的你。

78
00:07:40,258 --> 00:07:48,925
If you want to see the breaks, fix the cells about thirty minutes after irradiation. That is when the flares are most numerous.
想看断口，就在照射后约 30 分钟固定细胞，那是信号弹最多的时候。

79
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:58,708
If you want to see what the cell failed to repair, fix them again at twenty-four hours. The dots that remain are called residual foci.
想看细胞没修好的部分，就在 24 小时再固定一次。那时剩下的亮点，叫残余灶。

80
00:07:59,158 --> 00:08:10,585
Stain a second marker, such as 53BP1, at the same time. When the two dots sit on top of each other, you can trust that you are looking at a real break.
同时再染一个标记，比如 53BP1。两种亮点叠在一起时，你才能相信看到的是真正的断口。

81
00:08:11,035 --> 00:08:17,503
And count the dots without knowing which dish is which. The eye sees what it hopes to see.
还有，数亮点时不要知道哪皿是哪组。眼睛总会看到它希望看到的东西。

82
00:08:20,953 --> 00:08:26,835
A break is like a breach in a levee. First, someone must sound the alarm.
断口就像河堤上的缺口。首先，得有人拉响警报。

83
00:08:27,285 --> 00:08:31,863
In the cell, that sentry is a protein kinase called ATM.
在细胞里，这名哨兵是一种叫 ATM 的蛋白激酶。

84
00:08:32,313 --> 00:08:40,587
It senses the break, and it marks proteins all around it, including H2AX and the guardian protein p53.
它察觉断口，就给周围一连串蛋白打上标记，其中包括 H2AX 和守护者蛋白 p53。

85
00:08:41,037 --> 00:08:47,502
Stephen Jackson and Jiri Bartek described this whole response in a review in Nature in 2009.
Stephen Jackson 和 Jiri Bartek 在 2009 年的《自然》综述里，描述了这一整套反应。

86
00:08:47,952 --> 00:08:50,462
The alarm does three things at once.
这个警报同时做三件事。

87
00:08:50,912 --> 00:08:57,094
It stops the cell cycle at checkpoints, so the cell does not copy or divide a broken blueprint.
第一，让细胞周期在检查点停下，免得细胞去复制或分裂一份断裂的蓝图。

88
00:08:57,544 --> 00:08:59,673
It calls in the repair crews.
第二，召来修复队。

89
00:09:00,123 --> 00:09:06,097
And if the damage is too great, it prepares the cell to die, or to stop working for good.
第三，如果损伤太重，就让细胞准备死亡，或者永久停工。

90
00:09:06,547 --> 00:09:08,403
There are two main crews.
主要的修复队有两支。

91
00:09:08,853 --> 00:09:15,556
The first is called non-homologous end joining, or NHEJ. It is a fast welder.
第一支叫非同源末端连接，简称 NHEJ。它是一位快手焊工。

92
00:09:16,006 --> 00:09:23,543
A ring-shaped protein called Ku grabs the two broken ends, the ends are trimmed if needed, and they are sealed together.
一种叫 Ku 的环形蛋白抓住两个断端，必要时修修边，再把它们封接起来。

93
00:09:23,993 --> 00:09:30,120
The welder works at any time of the cell's life, but it can lose or change a few letters at the seam.
这位焊工在细胞一生的任何时候都能干活，但接缝处可能丢掉或改动几个字母。

94
00:09:30,570 --> 00:09:37,353
The second crew is homologous recombination, or HR. It is a careful scribe.
第二支叫同源重组，简称 HR。它是一位细心的抄写员。

95
00:09:37,803 --> 00:09:41,684
It copies the missing text from the sister copy of the DNA.
它从 DNA 的姐妹副本那里，照抄缺失的内容。

96
00:09:42,134 --> 00:09:49,457
But that sister copy exists only after the cell has duplicated its chromosomes, in preparation for dividing.
可这份姐妹副本，只在细胞为分裂做准备、复制完染色体之后才存在。

97
00:09:49,907 --> 00:09:54,207
So the scribe is available only to cells that are getting ready to divide.
所以，只有准备分裂的细胞才请得动这位抄写员。

98
00:09:54,657 --> 00:10:00,868
Howard Chang and his colleagues, writing in 2017, explained why this matters.
Howard Chang 等人在 2017 年的综述里解释了这为什么重要。

99
00:10:01,318 --> 00:10:08,975
A cell that is resting, not preparing to divide, has no sister copy. It must rely on the welder.
一个处于静息、不准备分裂的细胞，没有姐妹副本，只能依靠焊工。

100
00:10:09,425 --> 00:10:16,064
Hold on to that point. The muscle stem cells we will meet spend most of their lives resting.
请记住这一点：我们将要认识的肌肉干细胞，一生大部分时间都在静息。

101
00:10:16,514 --> 00:10:18,335
And here is a surprise.
这里有个出人意料的地方。

102
00:10:18,785 --> 00:10:27,512
In 2014, Leyla Vahidi Ferdousi and her colleagues irradiated muscle stem cells, and also their committed offspring.
2014 年，Leyla Vahidi Ferdousi 和同事照射了肌肉干细胞，也照射了它们已经定向分化的子代细胞。

103
00:10:27,962 --> 00:10:34,839
The resting stem cells repaired their double-strand breaks faster and more accurately than their more mature offspring.
静息的干细胞修复双链断裂，比更成熟的子代细胞更快、也更准确。

104
00:10:35,289 --> 00:10:40,466
They did it with the welder's key tool, a kinase called DNA-PKcs.
它们靠的是焊工的关键工具，一种叫 DNA-PKcs 的激酶。

105
00:10:40,916 --> 00:10:42,982
They also died less often.
它们的凋亡也更少。

106
00:10:43,432 --> 00:10:49,449
And the finished muscle nuclei, the ones that will never divide again, repaired worst of all.
而那些永不再分裂的成熟肌核，修复得最差。

107
00:10:49,899 --> 00:10:52,458
So the stem cells are good at repair.
所以，干细胞很擅长修复。

108
00:10:52,908 --> 00:10:57,221
Keep that in mind, because by the end of tonight it will become a puzzle.
请记住这一点，因为到今晚结束时，它会变成一道谜题。

109
00:10:57,671 --> 00:11:02,917
Another note from the bench. How would you prove which crew a cell is using?
再来一段实验台笔记：你怎么证明，一个细胞用的是哪支修复队？

110
00:11:03,367 --> 00:11:06,928
One way is to block a crew and watch what happens.
一种办法是：拦住其中一支，看看会发生什么。

111
00:11:07,378 --> 00:11:17,083
Give a drug that inhibits DNA-PKcs, and if the gamma-H2AX dots now linger for many more hours, the welder was doing the work.
给一种抑制 DNA-PKcs 的药物，如果 γH2AX 亮点因此多停留了好几个小时，说明干活的正是焊工。

112
00:11:17,533 --> 00:11:22,839
Inhibitors of ATM can be used the same way, to test the alarm itself.
ATM 抑制剂也可以这样用，来检验警报本身。

113
00:11:23,289 --> 00:11:33,470
But be careful. Drugs can hit more than one target, so a good experiment confirms the answer a second way, for example with a genetic knockdown.
但要小心：药物可能不止打中一个靶点。好的实验会用第二种方法确认答案，比如基因敲低。

114
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:42,417
After the alarm and the repair crews, every damaged cell comes to a fork in the road.
警报响过、修复队干完活之后，每个受伤的细胞都会走到一个岔路口。

115
00:11:42,867 --> 00:11:46,442
If the breaks are mended, it carries on with its life.
断口修好了，它就继续过日子。

116
00:11:46,892 --> 00:11:49,903
If the damage is too great, it may die.
损伤太重，它就可能死亡。

117
00:11:50,353 --> 00:11:54,571
It may choose an orderly suicide, called apoptosis.
它可能选择一种有序的自杀，叫凋亡。

118
00:11:55,021 --> 00:12:02,602
Or it may try to divide with broken chromosomes and fail, which radiation biologists call mitotic catastrophe.
也可能带着断裂的染色体硬去分裂而失败，放射生物学家称之为有丝分裂灾难。

119
00:12:03,052 --> 00:12:08,108
That means many cells die not at once, but the next time they try to divide.
这意味着很多细胞不是当场死去，而是在下一次试图分裂时才死。

120
00:12:08,558 --> 00:12:16,361
Tissues that divide often show their injuries early. Tissues that divide rarely may hide them for a long time.
常分裂的组织，伤情暴露得早；很少分裂的组织，伤情可能藏很久。

121
00:12:16,811 --> 00:12:25,148
The third road is the strangest. The cell neither repairs fully, nor dies. It freezes.
第三条路最奇特：细胞既没彻底修好，也没死去，而是冻住了。

122
00:12:25,598 --> 00:12:29,695
It stops dividing forever, yet it stays alive and busy.
它永远不再分裂，却仍然活着、仍在忙碌。

123
00:12:30,145 --> 00:12:37,806
This is called senescence, and one of its brakes is a protein called p21, which p53 switches on.
这叫做衰老，它的刹车之一是一种叫 p21 的蛋白，由 p53 打开。

124
00:12:38,256 --> 00:12:43,306
For a long time, people thought such frozen cells were simply out of the game.
很长一段时间里，人们以为这些冻住的细胞就是出局了。

125
00:12:43,756 --> 00:12:49,341
In 2008, Jean-Philippe Coppé and his colleagues showed that they are not quiet at all.
2008 年，Jean-Philippe Coppé 和同事证明，它们一点也不安静。

126
00:12:49,791 --> 00:12:54,808
They pour out inflammatory signals and enzymes that change the cells around them.
它们向外倾倒炎症信号和各种酶，改变周围的细胞。

127
00:12:55,258 --> 00:13:00,461
This was named the senescence-associated secretory phenotype, or SASP.
这被命名为衰老相关分泌表型，简称 SASP。

128
00:13:00,911 --> 00:13:07,255
Think of it as a rumor mill. One frozen cell can spread trouble through a whole neighborhood.
你可以把它想成一个谣言工厂：一个冻住的细胞，就能把麻烦散播到整个街区。

129
00:13:07,705 --> 00:13:17,304
Coppé's work was not done in muscle. But remember the rumor mill, because in episode three we will find one inside irradiated muscle.
Coppé 的工作不是在肌肉里做的。但请记住这个谣言工厂，因为第三集里，我们会在受照的肌肉里找到一个。

130
00:13:17,754 --> 00:13:21,162
This brings us to why the damage can appear so late.
这就引出了一个问题：损伤为什么能出现得这么晚？

131
00:13:21,612 --> 00:13:25,516
Radiation injury to normal tissue comes in two waves.
辐射对正常组织的伤害分两波到来。

132
00:13:25,966 --> 00:13:32,637
Early effects arrive within weeks, in tissues that renew quickly, like skin and the lining of the gut.
早期效应在几周内出现，发生在更新快的组织里，比如皮肤和肠道黏膜。

133
00:13:33,087 --> 00:13:39,267
Late effects arrive months or years later: scarring, stiffness, shrinking of the tissue.
晚期效应在几个月甚至几年后才出现：瘢痕、僵硬、组织萎缩。

134
00:13:39,717 --> 00:13:46,069
In 2006, Søren Bentzen argued that late injury is not just a pile of dead cells.
2006 年，Søren Bentzen 提出：晚期损伤不只是一堆死细胞。

135
00:13:46,519 --> 00:13:56,620
It is an active process that keeps running long after treatment, with cytokines, scarring signals, and slow damage to small blood vessels working together.
它是一个在治疗结束很久之后仍在运转的主动过程，细胞因子、促瘢痕信号和小血管的缓慢损伤一起起作用。

136
00:13:57,070 --> 00:14:04,157
That is why the woman in our story could walk out of the clinic healthy, and only years later feel the stairs grow steep.
这就是为什么我们故事里的那位女士走出诊所时好好的，几年后才觉得楼梯变陡了。

137
00:14:04,607 --> 00:14:10,057
And one more note from the bench. How do you tell which road a cell has taken?
再加一段实验台笔记：怎么判断一个细胞走了哪条路？

138
00:14:10,507 --> 00:14:21,453
For death by apoptosis, laboratories often stain for cleaved caspase-3, or use the TUNEL method, which marks the chopped DNA of dying cells.
判断凋亡，实验室常染剪切型 caspase-3，或者用 TUNEL 法，它会标记濒死细胞被剁碎的 DNA。

139
00:14:21,903 --> 00:14:35,509
For senescence, there is a blue stain called senescence-associated beta-galactosidase, and there are markers such as p21, p16, and the loss of a dividing marker called Ki-67.
判断衰老，有一种蓝色染色叫衰老相关 β-半乳糖苷酶，还有 p21、p16 等标记，以及增殖标记 Ki-67 的消失。

140
00:14:35,959 --> 00:14:45,000
No single marker is proof of senescence. A careful detective asks for at least two or three witnesses who tell the same story.
没有哪一个标记能单独证明衰老。细心的侦探至少要找两三个说法一致的证人。

141
00:14:48,450 --> 00:14:50,426
Now we come at last to muscle.
现在，我们终于来到肌肉。

142
00:14:50,876 --> 00:14:53,523
A muscle fiber is a remarkable thing.
肌纤维是个了不起的东西。

143
00:14:53,973 --> 00:15:00,970
It is one enormous cell, sometimes centimeters long, made by hundreds of precursor cells fusing together.
它是一个巨大的细胞，有时长达几厘米，由成百上千个前体细胞融合而成。

144
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:07,653
So a single fiber holds hundreds of nuclei, lined up along its length like lamps along a street.
所以一根纤维里有数百个细胞核，沿着它排开，像街边的一盏盏路灯。

145
00:15:08,103 --> 00:15:11,194
And once it is built, it never divides again.
而且一旦建成，它就再也不分裂了。

146
00:15:11,644 --> 00:15:19,112
Remember the second road, death by failed division. A cell that never divides cannot walk that road.
还记得第二条路吗？分裂失败而死。一个永不分裂的细胞，根本走不上那条路。

147
00:15:19,562 --> 00:15:25,018
So you might guess that the muscle fiber is hard to kill. And you would be right.
所以你也许会猜，肌纤维很难被杀死。你猜对了。

148
00:15:25,468 --> 00:15:31,682
In 2004, Lucia Latella, Jiri Bartek and their colleagues set up a fair test.
2004 年，Lucia Latella、Jiri Bartek 和同事设计了一场公平的比试。

149
00:15:32,132 --> 00:15:37,459
They gave ten grays to young muscle cells that could still divide, the myoblasts.
他们给还能分裂的年轻肌肉细胞，也就是成肌细胞，照射 10 Gy。

150
00:15:37,909 --> 00:15:43,337
And they gave the same dose to finished, fused muscle cells, the myotubes.
又给已经融合成型的肌肉细胞，也就是肌管，照射同样的剂量。

151
00:15:43,787 --> 00:15:51,120
Both raised the alarm. ATM switched on, and the gamma-H2AX flares lit up.
两者都拉响了警报：ATM 被激活，γH2AX 信号弹亮起。

152
00:15:51,570 --> 00:16:00,110
But in the myotubes, the message from ATM to p53 was blocked, and the myotubes did not undergo apoptosis.
可是在肌管里，ATM 传给 p53 的消息被拦住了，肌管没有发生凋亡。

153
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:03,609
The young myoblasts were far more vulnerable.
年轻的成肌细胞则脆弱得多。

154
00:16:04,059 --> 00:16:08,224
The authors called this differentiation-induced radioresistance.
作者把这叫做“分化诱导的放射抵抗”。

155
00:16:08,674 --> 00:16:17,905
These were cells in a dish, from mouse and from human. But the lesson was clear: becoming a finished fiber is a kind of armor.
这些是来自小鼠和人的培养细胞。但结论很清楚：长成一根成熟的纤维，本身就是一种盔甲。

156
00:16:18,355 --> 00:16:23,790
A study in 2024 carried this question into human tissue grown in a dish.
2024 年的一项研究，把这个问题带进了培养皿里生长的人类组织。

157
00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:29,440
The researchers built small three-dimensional muscle organoids from human stem cells.
研究者用人类干细胞，做成了小小的三维肌肉类器官。

158
00:16:29,890 --> 00:16:39,582
After two grays, the gamma-H2AX flares were brightest in the cells carrying PAX7, the badge worn by muscle stem cells.
照射 2 Gy 后，γH2AX 信号弹在带有 PAX7 的细胞里最亮，而 PAX7 正是肌肉干细胞佩戴的徽章。

159
00:16:40,032 --> 00:16:45,070
By seventy-two hours, the flares had faded, as repair did its work.
到 72 小时，随着修复完成，这些亮点已经消退。

160
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:49,937
A last note from the bench, because the next episode will lean on it.
最后一段实验台笔记，因为下一集要用到它。

161
00:16:50,387 --> 00:16:58,053
In the living animal, scientists irradiate one hind leg and shield the other, so each mouse carries its own control.
在活体动物身上，科学家常常只照射一条后腿，把另一条遮挡起来，这样每只小鼠都自带对照。

162
00:16:58,503 --> 00:17:07,459
Then they cut thin sections of the muscle, stain for PAX7, and count the stem cells per hundred fibers, or per square millimeter.
然后把肌肉切成薄片，染 PAX7，数每一百根纤维或每平方毫米里有多少干细胞。

163
00:17:07,909 --> 00:17:15,568
Weeks or months later, they measure fiber size and scar tissue, and sometimes the force the muscle can still produce.
几周或几个月后，再测纤维粗细和瘢痕组织，有时还会测这块肌肉还能发出多大的力。

164
00:17:16,018 --> 00:17:18,500
So let us lay the evidence on the table.
那就把证据摊在桌上。

165
00:17:18,950 --> 00:17:21,403
The muscle fiber survives the beam.
肌纤维挺过了射线。

166
00:17:21,853 --> 00:17:25,176
Yet years later, the muscle is thin and stiff.
可是几年以后，这块肌肉又细又硬。

167
00:17:25,626 --> 00:17:29,678
If the fiber did not fall, then somebody else paid the price.
既然纤维没有倒下，那就一定是别人付出了代价。

168
00:17:30,128 --> 00:17:36,697
Our prime suspects are the small cells that rebuild muscle, the cells that wear the PAX7 badge.
我们的头号嫌疑人，是那些负责重建肌肉、佩戴 PAX7 徽章的小细胞。

169
00:17:37,147 --> 00:17:39,487
And here is the puzzle I promised you.
而这，就是我答应过你的那道谜题。

170
00:17:39,937 --> 00:17:43,613
We just heard that those resting stem cells are good at repair.
我们刚听说，那些静息的干细胞很擅长修复。

171
00:17:44,063 --> 00:17:47,518
How, then, could radiation empty their ranks?
那么，射线又怎么可能让它们的队伍空掉呢？

172
00:17:47,968 --> 00:17:56,717
To answer that, we must go back to the year 1961, to an electron microscope, and to a cell that was hiding in plain sight.
要回答它，我们得回到 1961 年，回到一台电子显微镜前，去看一个就藏在眼皮底下的细胞。

173
00:17:57,167 --> 00:18:01,261
It was pressed against the muscle fiber like a sentry against a wall.
它紧贴着肌纤维，就像哨兵贴着城墙。

174
00:18:01,711 --> 00:18:04,799
In the next episode, we meet the guardians.
下一集，我们去见守护者。

175
00:18:05,249 --> 00:18:10,455
Until then, my friends, keep your questions sharp, and your evidence sharper.
在那之前，朋友们，让你的问题保持锋利，让你的证据更锋利。

176
00:18:10,905 --> 00:18:14,118
The references for tonight's episode are on the screen.
本集的参考文献，都在屏幕上。

